# A pattern without a leading slash matches at EVERY depth, not just at the
# root. That is how `runs/` — meant for the persisted run archive — also
# matched `core/runs/`, and kept the run store, the orchestration and the
# golden snapshots out of the repository for two entire phases without a
# single error message. Anything that describes a specific directory of THIS
# repository is therefore anchored with a leading slash; only patterns that
# legitimately apply at any depth (tool caches, build metadata, file
# extensions) are left unanchored.

# -- tool artefacts, legitimately at any depth ---------------------------
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.vscode/
.idea/
node_modules/

# -- secrets and machine-local files -------------------------------------
.env
*.log
*.pkl

# -- root-only directories, anchored -------------------------------------
# Each of these would otherwise swallow a package of the same name:
# `core/build/`, `core/dist/` (distributions, in a repo full of them),
# `core/logs/`, `core/runs/` — the one that actually happened.
/.venv/
/venv/
/build/
/dist/
/logs/
/.temp/

# -- regenerable data, heavy ---------------------------------------------
/data_cache/
/runs/
*.hc
# Cached bar data. The two directories above already hold all of it; this
# extension rule is the belt to their braces, and the reason a future test
# fixture must not be stored as .parquet.
*.parquet

# -- frontend build output -----------------------------------------------
/ui/dist/
